When the same instance supplies demonstrations with widely different dimensions, background colors, seed colors, and motif shapes, do not assume the visible seed in the test is enough to derive a familiar operation. First record each pair's input/output changed-cell mask, foreground/background roles, and unchanged seed/background cells. Search for one role-level invariant that explains all pairs; explicitly reject candidate rules such as zero-component flood fill or global recoloring unless they reproduce every output exactly.

After each failed submission, revisit the transformation hypothesis rather than changing arbitrary thresholds or component definitions. If the demo cap is exhausted, use remaining submissions only for a rule supported by the full evidence. This instance's guesses of connected-background recoloring were not demonstrated by cross-pair replay and failed; the reusable lesson is to validate cross-demo invariance before committing, not to favor another particular rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md", "content": "# Selecting demos for heterogeneous ARC examples

When available demonstrations differ sharply in grid size, palette, and object structure, log each pair's role colors, exact changed-cell mask, untouched/identity regions, and any plausible structural trigger. Treat a rule as supported only if it reproduces complete outputs, not merely the salient changed motif.

Before requesting another pair, name the competing hypotheses and the concrete observation that would distinguish them; ask only if that distinction can materially change the test prediction. Repeated requests without a targeted discriminator have cost but may not improve inference. In the same way, do not progress from one vivid example directly to a large speculative output: preserve the test input unless evidence supports a transformation. Keep this evidence-gathering discipline compatible with strict interfaces: each puzzle response still emits exactly one action JSON.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md", "content": "# Cross-demo changed-mask audit

When demos appear visually unrelated (different dimensions, background colors, seed motifs, and output colors), first compare their exact input/output differences rather than assuming a fixed motif-copy rule. For every pair, record: (1) role-level colors (background, seed, painted color), (2) exact changed-cell coordinates and their bounding boxes, (3) cells left unchanged inside apparent motifs, (4) any connected components or repeated offsets involved, and (5) a concrete rule that predicts both changed and unchanged cells.

A plausible hypothesis is not validated by resemblance. Replay it across *all* demos and require exact agreement, including untouched noise and cells that retain the background. In this session, several demos had completely different grid sizes and seed shapes, and a guessed connected-zero flood-fill failed; that illustrates why a visual/connectedness guess must be checked against demo outputs before submission. After a failed answer, re-evaluate the rule itself and seek a demo that distinguishes explicit alternatives, rather than making another unsupported guess. Keep each demo request tied to a named uncertainty, since requests and wrong submissions both incur cost.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md", "content": "# Attempt-cap handling and retry gate

In strict one-action puzzle interfaces, the protocol remains binding after the puzzle ends. When feedback says the instance is over—especially after exhausting the attempt cap—respond with exactly `{"action":"finish"}`. An empty assistant response or another `submit` is not a valid terminal action.

After a wrong submission, binary feedback gives no location information. Do not respond with a cosmetic or arbitrary variant (for example, changing only a suspected region's color). State the proposed model correction internally and test it against the complete demonstrations, including unchanged pairs and preserved cells. Only submit when the revised rule has evidence and reproduces all examples. If ambiguity remains and a demo is available, request one only to resolve a specific competing hypothesis; once demos are exhausted, further unsupported guesses compound cost and risk consuming the cap.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/post-rejection-audit.md", "content": "# Post-rejection audit and demo budgeting

Binary incorrect feedback rejects only the submitted output; it does not localize errors or validate a nearby alternative. Before another action, rebuild the evidence from all demonstrations, including no-op and visually heterogeneous pairs. Record role colors, exact changed and preserved cells, candidate rule, and whether the rule reproduces every demo. Identify the specific model correction being tested and the evidence for it.

A demo request and wrong submission each cost one point, so use a request when it can distinguish materially different hypotheses. Tie each request to a named unresolved distinction. If there is no evidence-based candidate that replays every example, prefer targeted information gathering over cycling through speculative variants. Keep the strict action protocol intact; do not add explanatory text to puzzle turns.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/post-rejection-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/case-study-cross-motif-inference.md", "content": "# Cross-motif inference lesson

A recurring failure mode is guessing a motif's footprint or anchor from an incomplete visual impression. Before submitting, extract the exact colored motif from the input, list its non-background cells as offsets from a chosen anchor, and infer how the anchor corresponds to each scattered marker. Then apply the proposed rule to all markers in the demo and reproduce every output cell, including preserved cells and clipping at boundaries. Use demos to resolve orientation/anchor ambiguity rather than repeatedly submitting speculative grids. Distinct instances may share an abstract operation while using different motifs, so transfer the operation but relearn each motif's geometry and anchor from its own examples.

Also audit output dimensions and row lengths mechanically when possible; a syntactically plausible grid may fail the ARC grid schema if any row differs in length.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/case-study-cross-motif-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md", "content": "# Periodic-pattern transfer from sparse marker grids

A sparse input may encode a palette/order and phase for a periodic output, rather than a local fill operation. Read the output demo as a 2D sequence: identify the minimal period, row-to-row phase shift, and top-left phase. Verify exact candidate mappings cellwise; common alternatives include `(r+c) mod p` and `(c-r) mod p`, which may look alike but diverge on asymmetric patterns.

Do not transfer a periodic sequence from one compelling demo unless the same role-level rule reproduces every example. No-op pairs and outputs with apparently different palettes/periods are evidence that the operation may be conditional or that the current abstraction is wrong. Following an incorrect submission, binary feedback cannot pinpoint phase versus palette errors. Avoid arbitrary variant cycling; request a discriminating demo while available, and if capped, make one best-supported choice rather than repeat rejected outputs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-generalization.md", "content": "# Cross-demo generalization under diverse examples

Continual-ARC demos can vary sharply in grid size, palette, and the seed's shape or location while still instantiating an abstract common operation. Do not seek one literal template that can be copied unchanged from the first example. For each pair, record (1) background and marker roles, (2) exact seed footprint including background-valued cells, (3) output additions and any removals, (4) placement/alignment rule, and (5) whether unrelated cells are preserved. Compare these role-level summaries across every pair.

Validate competing hypotheses by predicting the complete output for each demo. Similar-looking seed colors are not proof that every marker receives a stamp; check changed-cell masks and unchanged regions. If no single rule explains all examples, request another demo while budget remains rather than submitting a candidate that only resembles one pair. Once the rule is supported, apply it mechanically to the test input and audit the serialized grid separately.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-generalization.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-role-abstraction.md", "content": "# Reconciling diverse demonstrations

Continual-ARC demos can vary radically in grid size, palette, and apparent motif. Do not conclude they are unrelated solely from those differences, and do not unify them based on superficial shared colors or density. For each pair, extract the exact input-output changes and describe the transformation in role-level terms (background, marker/seed, copied footprint, output color). Compare those abstractions across all pairs. A compelling candidate must reproduce every demo cell-for-cell, including cells left unchanged and explicit background-valued positions in any stamped footprint. If no common validated rule emerges, another demo is more valuable than a speculative submission when the remaining budget permits.

A useful audit table records per demo: dimensions; color roles; seed/motif offsets; changed cells; proposed abstract operation; exact replay result. Distinguish a genuinely conditional rule from an incorrect attempt to force one unconditional template. Keep the final action protocol exact regardless of analysis length.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-role-abstraction.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/sparse-grid-demo-lesson.md", "content": "# Sparse-grid line completion lesson

In a sparse grid with scattered same-color marks, a demo may reveal that only specific aligned interior cells are painted a new color. The useful analysis is cellwise: record changed coordinates, separate changed background cells from original marks, then test row and column runs and their endpoints. Do not infer that every possible aligned pair is filled; verify every qualifying and non-qualifying run in the example. Transfer the smallest rule that exactly reproduces the full demo, preserving all original cells unless the output demonstrates replacement. In the reviewed instance, the test transformation was to paint background cells lying strictly between vertically aligned 0 markers using color 3, with marks and other cells preserved. First demo request gave no validation of this rule; the second demonstrated inserted 3s in line interiors. Exact output feedback confirmed the derived test answer.

Treat this as an evidence-backed pattern, not a universal ARC rule: obtain/compare examples to establish axis, endpoint, and color conditions before applying.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/sparse-grid-demo-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/periodic-stripe-sequence.md", "content": "# Periodic stripe grids: global-sequence hypothesis

When demos are made of uniform-color rows or columns, reason over the 1D stripe sequence as well as individual cells. Compare the input stripe sequence with the full output sequence, including repeated periods and boundary phase. A transformation may generate a periodic color sequence from a compact set of stripe exemplars rather than preserve or locally extend the visible rows. Extract the exact sequence from every demo, test candidate periods and phase, and verify every output stripe before transferring to the test. Do not extrapolate from a single visually suggestive sample; request another demo if the rule connecting input stripe order to output period remains ambiguous.
